=> DBMS Prerequisites – MySQL Complete Installation and Configuration

    -> Prerequisites
        -> Operating System: Windows 10/11 (64-bit)
        -> Database Server: MySQL Community Server
        -> Graphical Client: MySQL Workbench
        -> Command-Line Client: MySQL Shell
        -> Administrator Access: Required for server installation and service configuration
        -> Internet: Required to download the installation files

    -> Understanding the Three Tools
        -> MySQL Server
            -> Actual database engine responsible for storing and managing data.
            -> Executes SQL queries submitted by database clients.
            -> Can run continuously as a Windows background service.

        -> MySQL Workbench
            -> Graphical user interface for MySQL.
            -> Used to write SQL queries, design tables, and create ER diagrams.
            -> Connects to MySQL Server.

        -> MySQL Shell
            -> Command-line interface for interacting with MySQL Server.
            -> Supports SQL, JavaScript, and Python execution modes.
            -> Useful for terminal-based SQL practice.
            -> Different from the Linux operating system shell.

    -> Basic Architecture

        MySQL Workbench (GUI)
                  |
                  | SQL Queries
                  v
             MySQL Server
                  ^
                  | SQL Queries
                  |
        MySQL Shell (CLI)

        -> MySQL Server manages the data.
        -> Workbench and Shell are separate clients.
        -> Both can connect to the same database server.

    -> Step 1: Download MySQL Server
        -> Official Website: https://dev.mysql.com/downloads/mysql/
        -> Select Microsoft Windows.
        -> Prefer a supported LTS release for a stable learning environment.
        -> Select Windows (x86, 64-bit), MSI Installer.
        -> Download the installation package.
        -> MSI is recommended for beginners because it simplifies setup.
        -> ZIP archives require manual server configuration.

    -> Step 2: Install MySQL Server Using MSI
        -> Run the downloaded MSI installer.
        -> Follow the installation wizard.
        -> Launch MySQL Configurator when prompted.
        -> Configure MySQL for local development.
        -> Enable TCP/IP connectivity.
        -> Default MySQL classic protocol port: 3306.
        -> Create a strong password for the root account.
        -> Store the password securely.
        -> Configure MySQL Server as a Windows service.
        -> Enable automatic startup if required.
        -> Apply configuration and start the server.
        -> Complete the setup wizard.

    -> Alternative: MySQL ZIP Installation
        -> Extract the ZIP archive to a permanent location.
        -> Example: C:\software\mysql-9.7.2-winx64
        -> Locate the bin directory containing mysqld.exe.
        -> Configure the data directory and server options.
        -> Initialize a new database data directory securely.
        -> Configure authentication and the root password.
        -> Install or start the MySQL Windows service.
        -> Initialization commands depend on the extracted layout.
        -> Never initialize an existing database data directory again.

    -> Step 3: Verify MySQL Server Installation
        -> Open Windows Services using services.msc.
        -> Find the configured MySQL service.
        -> Example service name: MySQL97.
        -> Verify that the service status is Running.
        -> Service names may vary.

        -> Optional CMD Verification
            -> Execute: mysql --version
            -> Requires the MySQL client executable to be installed and accessible through PATH.
            -> Displays the client version, not necessarily the connected server version.

    -> Step 4: Download and Install MySQL Workbench
        -> Official Website: https://dev.mysql.com/downloads/workbench/
        -> Select Microsoft Windows.
        -> Download the Windows 64-bit MSI installer.
        -> Run the installer.
        -> Select Typical installation if available.
        -> Complete the installation.
        -> Open MySQL Workbench.
        -> Workbench and Server version numbers do not need to be identical.
        -> Compatibility limitations may exist between particular versions.

    -> Step 5: Configure MySQL Workbench Connection
        -> Open MySQL Workbench.
        -> Click the + icon under MySQL Connections.
        -> Enter the connection details.

            -> Connection Name: Local MySQL
            -> Connection Method: Standard (TCP/IP)
            -> Hostname: localhost
            -> Port: 3306
            -> Username: root
            -> Default Schema: Leave blank

        -> Click Test Connection.
        -> Enter the configured root password.
        -> After successful testing, save the connection.
        -> Double-click the connection to open SQL Editor.
        -> Execute the query:

            SELECT VERSION();

        -> Expected output: Connected MySQL Server version.
        -> Example: 9.7.2.

    -> Step 6: Download and Install MySQL Shell
        -> Official Website: https://dev.mysql.com/downloads/shell/
        -> Select Microsoft Windows.
        -> Download the Windows 64-bit MSI Installer.
        -> Run the installer and complete installation.
        -> Open Command Prompt.
        -> Execute:

            mysqlsh --version

        -> Expected output: Installed MySQL Shell version.
        -> Example: mysqlsh Ver 26.7.1 for Win64.

    -> Step 7: Connect MySQL Shell to MySQL Server
        -> Open MySQL Shell from the Windows Start menu.
        -> Execute:

            \connect root@localhost:3306

        -> Enter the root password when prompted.
        -> After successful connection, activate SQL mode.

            \sql

        -> Verify the server version.

            SELECT VERSION();

        -> Example result: 9.7.2.

    -> Step 8: MySQL Shell Execution Modes
        -> SQL Mode
            -> Used to execute SQL queries.
            -> Command: \sql

        -> JavaScript Mode
            -> Used for JavaScript scripting.
            -> Command: \js

        -> Python Mode
            -> Used for Python scripting.
            -> Command: \py

        -> Important Difference
            -> Commands beginning with \ are generally MySQL Shell commands.
            -> SQL statements are database commands.
            -> SQL statements generally end with a semicolon (;).

=> Basic MySQL Commands for Beginners

    -> Command 1: Connect to MySQL Server
        -> Command:
            \connect root@localhost:3306
        -> Purpose: Connect to the local MySQL Server.
        -> Enter the password when prompted.

    -> Command 2: Switch to SQL Mode
        -> Command:
            \sql
        -> Purpose: Enable SQL query execution.

    -> Command 3: Show Databases
        -> SQL:
            SHOW DATABASES;
        -> Purpose: Display databases visible to the connected user.

    -> Command 4: Create Database
        -> SQL:
            CREATE DATABASE dbms_practice;
        -> Purpose: Create a new database named dbms_practice.

    -> Command 5: Select Database
        -> SQL:
            USE dbms_practice;
        -> Purpose: Select the database for subsequent queries.

    -> Command 6: Create Table
        -> SQL:
            CREATE TABLE employees (
                emp_id INT PRIMARY KEY,
                emp_name VARCHAR(100),
                salary DECIMAL(10,2)
            );
        -> Purpose: Create a table containing employee details.
        -> emp_id: Primary key.
        -> emp_name: Employee name.
        -> salary: Employee salary.

    -> Command 7: Show Tables
        -> SQL:
            SHOW TABLES;
        -> Purpose: List the tables in the currently selected database.

    -> Command 8: Show Table Structure
        -> SQL:
            DESCRIBE employees;
        -> Purpose: Display columns, data types, keys, and other structural details.

    -> Command 9: Insert Data
        -> SQL:
            INSERT INTO employees (emp_id, emp_name, salary)
            VALUES (101, 'Amit', 45000.00);
        -> Purpose: Insert one employee record.

    -> Command 10: Retrieve Data
        -> SQL:
            SELECT * FROM employees;
        -> Purpose: Display every column and row in the employees table.

=> Additional MySQL Commands

    -> Check Current Database
        -> SQL:
            SELECT DATABASE();

    -> Check Current Database Account
        -> SQL:
            SELECT CURRENT_USER();

    -> Check Server Version
        -> SQL:
            SELECT VERSION();

    -> Check MySQL Shell Connection Status
        -> Command:
            \status

    -> Disconnect MySQL Server
        -> Command:
            \disconnect
        -> Disconnects the active global server connection.
        -> MySQL Shell remains open.

    -> Reconnect MySQL Server
        -> Command:
            \connect root@localhost:3306
        -> Enter the password when prompted.

    -> Exit MySQL Shell
        -> Command:
            \exit
        -> Closes MySQL Shell.
        -> Does not delete existing databases.

=> Practical Execution Flow

    -> Open MySQL Shell.
    -> Connect using \connect root@localhost:3306
    -> Switch to SQL mode using \sql
    -> Verify connection using SELECT VERSION();
    -> Display databases using SHOW DATABASES;
    -> Create database using CREATE DATABASE dbms_practice;
    -> Select database using USE dbms_practice;
    -> Create table using CREATE TABLE.
    -> Verify table using SHOW TABLES;
    -> Display table structure using DESCRIBE employees;
    -> Insert employee data using INSERT INTO.
    -> Verify inserted records using SELECT.
    -> Disconnect using \disconnect
    -> Exit using \exit

=> Common Errors and Troubleshooting

    -> Error: mysqlsh is not recognized
        -> Verify MySQL Shell installation.
        -> Check whether mysqlsh.exe is accessible in PATH.
        -> Reopen CMD after updating environment variables.

    -> Error: Cannot Connect to localhost:3306
        -> Verify MySQL Server is running.
        -> Check the configured Windows service.
        -> Verify hostname and port.

    -> Error: Access Denied for root
        -> Check the username.
        -> Verify the configured root password.
        -> Check whether the account is permitted to connect locally.

    -> Error: No Database Selected
        -> Execute:
            USE dbms_practice;

    -> Error: Table Already Exists
        -> Verify existing tables:
            SHOW TABLES;
        -> Do not recreate an existing table unnecessarily.

    -> Error: Table Doesn't Exist
        -> Check the selected database.
        -> Verify the table name.

    -> Error: Workbench Compatibility Warning
        -> Check Workbench and Server compatibility.
        -> Verify whether basic SQL connections work.
        -> Some advanced Workbench features may not support newer server versions.

=> Important Interview and Exam Points

    -> MySQL is an RDBMS.
    -> SQL is a language used to communicate with relational databases.
    -> MySQL Server is the database engine.
    -> MySQL Workbench is a graphical database client.
    -> MySQL Shell is a command-line database client.
    -> localhost refers to the current computer.
    -> Port 3306 is the default MySQL classic protocol port.
    -> MySQL clients can disconnect without shutting down the database server.
    -> Closing MySQL Shell does not delete stored data.
    -> CREATE DATABASE is a DDL statement.
    -> CREATE TABLE is a DDL statement.
    -> INSERT is a DML statement.
    -> SELECT retrieves data and is commonly classified as DQL in introductory learning.
    -> PRIMARY KEY uniquely identifies rows and does not allow NULL values.
    -> For production applications, avoid using the root account for routine database operations.

